  "name": "Question 16",
  "problem": "Which of the following functions are injections?\n\nI. $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={x}^{3}$\n\nII. $g:R\\to R$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{2}$\n\nIII. $h:R\\to R$ defined by $h(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$",
  "worked_solution": "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **injection** (also known as **one-to-one**) if *every* element in $x$ in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y.$\n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- I is an injection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ {x}_{1}^{3} & ={x}_{2}^{3} \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{x}_{1}^{3}} & =\\sqrt[3]{{x}_{2}^{3}} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- II is *not*  an injection.  For example, $g(1)=1=g(-1),$ but $1\\ne -1.$\n\n- III is *not* an injection. For example, $h(0)=0=h(\\pi ),$ but $0\\ne \\pi .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\".",
